=== Working with Remotes
tag::concept[]

(((remote repositories)))
To be able to collaborate on any Git project, you need to know how to manage your remote repositories. Remote repositories are versions of your project that are hosted on the Internet or a network somewhere.

Collaborating with others involves pushing and pulling data to and from these remotes when you need to share work. While Nora is currently working solo on SketchSpark, she knows that she’ll soon need to share her research with Sam and Priya.

==== Showing Your Remotes
tag::procedure[]

(((git commands, remote)))
To see which remote servers you have configured, you can run the `git remote` command. If Nora has just initialized her project locally, she won’t see anything yet:

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote
----

However, if she had cloned the project from a server, she would see `origin`—the default name Git gives to the server you cloned from.

To see the URLs associated with each remote, use the `-v` flag:

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote -v
----

==== Adding Remote Repositories
tag::procedure[]

Suppose Nora sets up a private space on GitHub for the SketchSpark team. To add this as a remote she can reference easily, she runs:

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote add origin https://github.com/sketchspark/sketchspark.git
----

Now Nora can use the string `origin` instead of the whole URL whenever she wants to interact with the server.

==== Fetching and Pulling from Your Remotes
tag::procedure[]

(((git commands, fetch)))
(((git commands, pull)))
To get data from your remote project, you use `git fetch origin`. This downloads all the data from the server that you don't yet have. It’s important to note that `git fetch` only downloads the data—it doesn't automatically merge it with your current work. You have to do that manually.

If you want Git to both fetch and automatically merge the remote changes into your current branch, use `git pull`:

[source,bash]
----
$ git pull origin main
----

For designers, `git pull` is the morning ritual: "What did my teammates update while I was away?"

==== Pushing to Your Remotes
tag::procedure[]

(((git commands, push)))
When Nora has her research at a point where she wants to share it with the team (or just back it up to the cloud), she "pushes" her changes:

[source,bash]
----
$ git push origin main
----

This command works only if she has write access to the server and if nobody has pushed in the meantime. If Sam has already pushed a new layout, Nora’s push will be rejected until she pulls Sam’s work and integrates it.

==== Inspecting and Renaming Remotes
tag::reference[]

To see more information about a particular remote:

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote show origin
----

To rename a remote (for example, from `pb` to `paul`):

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote rename pb paul
----

To remove a remote you’re no longer using:

[source,bash]
----
$ git remote remove paul
----
